                %a{:href => "#presentations"} Presentations
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#model_categories"} Model Categories
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#generalized_morphisms"} Generalized Morphisms
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#quillen_equivalences"} Quillen Equivalences
              %li
                %a{:href => "#RelatedConcepts"} Related entries
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#flavors_of_homotopy_theory_2"} Flavors of homotopy theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#basic_concepts_in_homotopy_theory"} Basic concepts in homotopy theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#categorical_homotopy_theory"} Categorical homotopy theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#prehistory"} Pre-history
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesTopologicalHomotopyTheory"} Topological homotopy theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesAlegbraicTopology"} Algebraic topology
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesAbstractHomotopyTheory"} Abstract homotopy theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesSimplicialHomotopyTheory"} Simplicial homotopy theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesBasicInfinityCategoryTheory"}
                    Basic
                    = succeed "-category" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesBasicHomotopyTypeTheory"} Basic homotopy type theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesOutlook"} Outlook
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#GeneralIdea General idea
          %p
            In generality,
            %em homotopy theory
            is the study of mathematical contexts in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} functions
            or rather (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"}> homo
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphisms
            are equipped with a concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            between them, hence with a concept of “equivalent
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformations"} deformations
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 10px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/2Cell.jpg", :width => "200px"}/
          %p
            and then iteratively with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies+of+homotopies"} homotopies of homotopies
            between those:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 0px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/3Cell.jpg", :width => "215px"}/
          %p and so forth.
          %p
            A key aspect here is that in such homotopy theoretic contexts the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            is relaxed to that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            is regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+morphism"} invertible
            if there is a reverse function such that both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composites
            are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            one only requires the composites to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopic
            to the identity. Regarding objects in a homotopical context up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            this way is to regard them as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
          %p It is not wrong to summarize this by saying that:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %em>
                Homotopy theory embodies the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+principle"} gauge principle
                in mathematics
              \.
          %p
            Namely, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+principle"} gauge principle
            says that it is wrong to ask for any two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} things
            to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equal"} equal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            relating them, and then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge-of-gauge+transformation"} gauge-of-gauge transformation
            relating these, etc. Similarly in homotopy theory it is wrong to ask whether any two objects are equal, instead one has to ask whether there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            between them and then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopies"} higher homotopies
            between these, etc.
          %p
            %br/
          %p For more exposition on the general idea of homotopy theory see:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Higher+Structures"} Higher Structures
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#TopologicalHomotopyTheoryInIdeaSection Topological homotopy theory
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AHomotopy.jpg", :width => "380px"}/
          %p
            The archetypical example of a homotopy theory is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical homotopy theory of topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            between them, and with the original concept of
            %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Topology+--+2#LeftHomotopy"} topological homotopies
            between these continuous functions.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} classes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            is also called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+homotopy+category"} classical homotopy category
          %p
            Classical constructions in topological homotopy theory are
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toda+brackets"} Toda brackets
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequences+of+homotopy+groups"} long exact sequences of homotopy groups
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical homotopy theory of topological spaces
            has many applications, for example to
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
                theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology+theory"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology theory
            and many more. (See also at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %p
            %br/
          %p For more exposition of topological homotopy theory see:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Topology+--+2"} Introduction to Basic Homotopy Theory
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#abstract_homotopy_theory Abstract homotopy theory
          %p
            The basic structures seen in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical homotopy theory of topological spaces
            may be abstracted to yield an “abstract homotopy theory” that applies to a large variety of contexts. There are several more or less equivalent formalizations of the concept of “abstract homotopy theory”, including
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theories
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+observational+type+theories"} higher observational type theories
                \.
          %p
            The terminology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            is short for “category of models of homotopy types”. The idea here is to consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            equipped with suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} extra structure and properties
            that encodes the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            between all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            and convenient means to handle and control these, in particular a means to construct the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
          %p
            %br/
          %p For a detailed introduction to abstract homotopy theory via model cateories see:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Homotopy+Theory"} Introduction to Homotopy Theory
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+homotopy+types"} geometry of physics – homotopy types
                \.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The approach of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            to homotopy theory is meant to be more truthful to the intrinsic nature of homotopy theory. Instead of equipping an ordinary category with a extra concept of homotopy between its morphisms, here one regards the resulting structure as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category"} higher category
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            themselves appear as a kind of higher order morphisms, called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
            and where higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies+of+homotopies"} homotopies of homotopies
            are regarded as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphisms"} k-morphisms
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            The terminology “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> (∞,1)-category
            ” signifies that homotopy theory is but one special case of general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            (hence homotopy theories) are those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-categories"} infinity-categories
            in which all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphisms"} k-morphisms
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \gt 1
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+morphisms"} invertible
            up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy theory
          %p
            The archetypical example of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            is the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is the archetypical 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"}> category
            \.
          %p
            This turns out to be equivalent, as homotopy theories, to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical homotopy theory of topological spaces
            if restricted to those that admit the structure of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#RelationBetweenTopologicalAndAbstractHomotopyTheoryInIdeaSection Relation between topological and abstract homotopy theory: Cohesion.
          %p
            The case of the homotopy theory of topological spaces (
            %a{:href => "#TopologicalHomotopyTheoryInIdeaSection"}> above
            ) is so classical that often the distinction between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            and homotopy theory has been (certainly so in the early literature) and still is often blurred (for instance in textbook titles or lecture notes). A transparent conceptual way to understand how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            and homotopy theory are closely related and yet different is provided by the notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em has
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em presents
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            through the data that is retained in its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} path ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+set"} singular simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sing
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing(X)
            (which is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            and as such an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} path ∞-groupoid
            – the latter is just a shadow of it, called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            of the topological space.
          %p
            The extra information in a topological space is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of points: The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            information of how these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
            stick together within
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shapes
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"}> path ∞-groupoids
            ) in plain homotopy theory.
          %p More formally:
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-topological+spaces"} D-topological spaces
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} full subcategory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
              cohesive
              = succeed "-topos" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-topological+infinity-groupoid"}
              D-topological
              = succeed "-groupoids" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            (also of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoids"}
                smooth
                = succeed "-groupoids" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} qualitative aspect
            called their
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+via+cohesive+path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} generalization
            of the
            = succeed "-construction." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            as an object in (plain)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groupoid"} topological groupoid
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive homotopy theory
            and then, as such, to retain only its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            which is “presented” by the topological space:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 40px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/RelationTopologyHomotopyTheory_20210921.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#simplicial_homotopy_theory Simplicial homotopy theory
          %p
            The most immediate way to model an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            is as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            which is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical homotopy theory of simplicial sets
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"} simplicial homotopy theory
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#flavors_of_homotopy_theory Flavors of homotopy theory
          %p But there are many other homotopy theories besides (the various incarnations of) this classical one. Important sub-classes of homotopy theories include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
                modeled by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+model+categories"} stable model categories
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/looping+and+delooping"} looping and delooping
                are an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} stable (∞,1)-category of spectra
                as well as those of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves+of+spectra"} sheaves of spectra
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+theory"} geometric homotopy theory
                modeled by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+toposes"} model toposes
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+sheaves"} categories of sheaves
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-categories of (∞,1)-sheaves
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
                = succeed "\u201D." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} elementary (∞,1)-toposes
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+theory"} geometric homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            in particular serves as the foundation for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            are identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+theory"} geometric homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+homotopy+types"} geometry of physics – homotopy types
          %p
            On the other hand, the incarnation of homotopy theory as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            exhibits the remarkably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundational
            nature of homotopy theory. Contrary to its original appearance as a fairly complicated-looking theory built on top of classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            and classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            just by adopting a fully
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            attitude towards the concept of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            for more on this. For exposition of this perspective see (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman17"}> Shulman 17
            ).
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#presentations Presentations
          %p
            A convenient, powerful, and traditional way to deal with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            is to “present” them by 1-categories with specified classes of morphisms called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalences
            \: a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with a class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            of weak equivalences, we can form its
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            or
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+fractions"} category of fractions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[W^{-1}]
            by adjoining formal inverses to all the morphisms in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} (∞,1)-category presented by
            = succeed "\u201C" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            as an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\gt 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            in the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalences
            rather than isomorphisms. It is remarkable that most
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            As with presentations of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              = succeed "es," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complex"} CW-complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} Quillen model structure on topological spaces
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              = succeed "es," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              simplicial homotopy equivalences
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              weak homotopy equivalences - see
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} Quillen model structure on simplicial sets
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              functors whose nerves are weak homotopy equivalences – see
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+structure"} Thomason model structure
          %p The latter three can hence be regarded as providing “combinatorial models” for the homotopy theory of topological spaces.
          %h3#model_categories Model Categories
          %p
            The value of working with presentations of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "," do
              %em homotopically meaningful
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            Most presentations of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em fibrations
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em cofibrations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            to be a 1-category together with classes of morphisms called weak equivalences, cofibrations, and fibrations that fit together in a very precise way (the term is meant to suggest “a category of models for a homotopy theory”). Many, perhaps most, presentations of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            Unlike the weak equivalences, which determine the “homotopy theory” and the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em technical tools
            which make working directly with the presentation easier (or possible). Whether a morphism is a fibration or cofibration has no meaning after we pass to the presented
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em every
            morphism is weakly equivalent to a fibration and to a cofibration. In particular, despite the common use of double-headed arrows for fibrations and hooked arrows for cofibrations, they do not correspond to
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            In a model category, a morphism which is both a fibration and a weak equivalence is called an
            %strong acyclic fibration
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong trivial fibration
            %strong acyclic
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %strong trivial cofibrations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is called
            %strong cofibrant
            if the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to X
            from the initial object to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a cofibration, and
            %strong fibrant
            if the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to 1
            to the terminal# object is a fibration. The axioms of a model category ensure that for every object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            there is an acyclic fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q X \to X
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q X
            is cofibrant and an acyclic cofibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to R X
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R X
            is fibrant.
          %h3#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+homotopy+theory"}> topological homotopy theory
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"}> simplicial homotopy theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+homotopy+theory"} localic homotopy theory
          %p For a (higher) category theorist, the following examples of model categories are perhaps the most useful to keep in mind:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              sets,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              isomorphisms.
              %em All
              morphisms are both fibrations and cofibrations. The
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              categories,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              equivalences of categories. The cofibrations are the functors which are injective on objects, and the fibrations are the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibrations
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              = succeed "." do
                %em folk model structure
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=
              (strict) 2-categories and (strict) 2-functors,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=
              2-functors which are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalences of bicategories
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat
          %h3#generalized_morphisms Generalized Morphisms
          %p
            The morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            in the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W)
            are zigzags
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo →
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}
                    \\to
                    \\stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}
                    \\to
                    \\cdots
            = succeed "." do
              %strong generalized morphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ≃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow} \to \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Q
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mi Y
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow} Q X \to  R Y \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow} Y
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q X
            is cofibrant and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Y
            is fibrant. In this case we can moreover take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q X\to X
            to be an acyclic fibration and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to R Y
            to be an acyclic cofibration.
          %p
            Often it suffices to consider even shorter zigzags of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ≃
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow} \to
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ≃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              are strict 2-categories, then pseudofunctors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
              are equivalent to strict 2-functors
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q X \to Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q X
              is a cofibrant replacement for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctors
              are zigzags
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ≃
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow} \to
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/folk+model+structure"} folk model structure
              on 1-categories whose first factor is an acyclic (i.e. surjective) fibration.
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita morphisms
              in the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoids
              are generalized morphisms of length one where both maps are acyclic fibrations.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is cofibrant and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is fibrant, then every generalized morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is equivalent to an ordinary morphism. For example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a cofibrant 2-category, then every pseudofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            is equivalent to a strict 2-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
          %h3#quillen_equivalences Quillen Equivalences
          %p
            Quillen also introduced a highly structured notion of equivalence between model categories, now called a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            = succeed "-category." do
